  "answer_text": "Great question! Here is a comprehensive overview of what opioids are derived from:\n\n---\n\n## What Are Opioids Derived From?\n\nOpioids are a class of drugs derived from substances that occur naturally in the **opium poppy** (*Papaver somniferum*) and are widely used for pain relief and sedation. Based on their origin, opioids can be broken down into three main categories:\n\n---\n\n### 1. \ud83c\udf3f Natural Opioids\nNatural opioids are substances derived directly from the opium poppy, typically from the **resin of the plant's seed pod**. Natural opioids are also referred to as **opiates**. Natural opioids such as **codeine, heroin, morphine, and opium** can be extracted from the opium poppy.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \ud83e\uddea Semi-Synthetic Opioids\nSemi-synthetic opioids are made by **chemically processing natural opioids**, modifying them into new opioid compounds. While these drugs are man-made and don't occur in nature, natural opioids from the opium poppy serve as the \"raw material\" that manufacturers use to make them. Examples include **hydrocodone** (e.g., Vicodin), **hydromorphone** (e.g., Dilaudid), and **oxycodone** (e.g., OxyContin, Percocet), which may be partially synthesized from chemical components of opium.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \u2697\ufe0f Synthetic Opioids\nSynthetic opioids are **entirely man-made**. Unlike semi-synthetic opioids that are derived from compounds extracted from the opium poppy, synthetic opioids are made from other **precursor chemicals** that do not depend on the opium poppy. Synthetic opioid compounds are made to **mimic natural opioids** (as well as endorphins), allowing them to activate opioid receptors. Examples include **fentanyl, methadone, and tramadol**.\n\n---\n\n### A Brief History\nCultivation of the opium poppy for its active constituents is thought to have begun around **3000 BCE**, and opioids that occur naturally in the plant have been used specifically for relieving pain and producing euphoria since at least the time of the ancient Greeks. In the 20th century, experiments with opiates led to the development of synthetic compounds with similar effects on the body. To distinguish them from natural opiates, these synthetic compounds were called **opioids** (meaning \"opiate-like\").\n\n---\n\n### How They Work\nOpioids are a class of drugs that derive from, or mimic, natural substances found in the opium poppy plant, and they work in the brain to produce a variety of effects, including **pain relief**. However, opioid drugs, even those prescribed by a doctor, are very addictive and can lead to opioid use disorder. Using any opioids for their euphoric effect, even for a short time, raises the risk of substance use disorder, overdose, and death.",
